  "id": "people-feelings",
  "uuid": "56e231e9-0d56-5405-9a53-491415a17c13",
  "title": "Happy, Worried, Angry: Moods and Personalities",
  "category": "people",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 4,
  "order": 405,
  "theme": "people",
  "culturalNote": "Kenyans often describe feelings with \"niko na…\" — literally \"I am with…\": niko na furaha (I'm happy), niko na wasiwasi (I'm worried), niko na hasira (I'm angry). Describing people is just as common, and the words come in singular and plural pairs: mcheshi / wacheshi (a funny person / funny people), mvivu / wavivu (lazy). Calling someone \"mpole\" (gentle, calm) is a big compliment.",
  "startingPoint": "Friday evening. Otieno flops onto the sofa next to you in your shared flat.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "ko-location",
    "adjective-agreement",
    "possessives",
    "present-na"
  ],
